There’s a real structural rhyme between them: an intense drive organizes your attention around something; when the drive drops, the object can suddenly seem less compelling, and you’re left with a strange blank space.

	Post-awakening emptiness	“Post-nut clarity”
What may have shifted	A belief, identity, or old source of meaning has loosened.	Sexual arousal and the immediate pursuit of release have subsided.
What the emptiness feels like	“If that old story isn’t true, what do I live by now?”	“Why did I want that so badly a moment ago?”
Typical scale	Existential and potentially part of a longer reorganization.	Usually brief and tied to the body’s resolution and refractory period.
What it can show you	The old meaning system may no longer fit.	A desire can feel different once arousal is gone.

A small attribution note: “post-awakening emptiness” isn’t a standard Jungian term. Jung’s more relevant ideas are individuation and the risk of mistaking an ego insight for complete psychological wholeness; later Jungian writers also discuss the role of powerful, “numinous” experiences in that process.

And “post-nut clarity” is a folk phrase, not a reliable truth detector. People can have a range of feelings after sex or orgasm, and the physiology of the refractory period is still not fully understood. So the calmer judgment afterward may be useful, but it isn’t automatically wiser or more authentic.

My read is that the analogy works best as a change in state: the thing that felt charged loses its grip, and you can see it from a little distance. The difference is what follows. Post-nut clarity is often a temporary drop in a specific drive; the Jungian version points toward the harder question of what will organize your life after an old source of meaning has faded. That’s my interpretation of the comparison, rather than a claim Jung made directly.